The change introduces a distorted frame at supported viewport sizes and makes server-rendered artwork depend on successful hydration to become visible. The authorized two-per-group asynchronous preload policy is not a finding; its payload cost still needs the measured report required by the acceptance plan.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Preserve the metal frame’s aspect ratio — /Users/asmirnou/Projects/g/ramsider/app/globals.css:177-177
  On short phones and landscape viewports, the frame is forced to `width:100%` and a height derived independently from `100dvh`, then stretched with `object-fit:fill`. For example, at 390×664 the frame becomes roughly 390×508 (ratio 0.77), rather than its source ratio of 941×1628 (0.58), visibly distorting the metal artwork even though the inner photo is contained. The frame needs a proportional treatment that still meets the edge-to-edge requirement in [the acceptance plan](docs/media-loading-plan.md#request-and-acceptance-map).

- [P2] Avoid hiding server-rendered artwork until hydration — /Users/asmirnou/Projects/g/ramsider/app/globals.css:192-192
  When JavaScript is disabled, fails, or hydrates late, no code can add `data-media-ready`, so this rule hides every marked artwork image indefinitely—including the eager first tile. That makes the server-rendered first viewport blank despite the image being available. Keep the decode gate for the hydrated experience without making artwork visibility depend unconditionally on client execution.
